  "answer": "CDK1-dependent NUP98 phosphorylation initiates nuclear-import failure in sporadic ALS motor neurons\n\nHypothesis. In C9orf72-expansion-negative sporadic-ALS motor neurons that initially retain nuclear TDP-43, aberrant CDK1 activity phosphorylates NUP98, reduces its pore residence, and initiates nuclear-import failure; nuclear TDP-43 depletion then contributes to neuronal dysfunction. Initiation means a proximal transport trigger, not the first cause of ALS. CDK1 activation, the relevant sites, altered residence, and downstream mediation are speculative bridges, not established findings.\n\nEvidence and target-selection weakness. TDP-43 pathology disrupts nucleoporin localization and transport [S1]. Granule-independent TDP-43 assemblies recruit transport-associated proteins and accompany import impairment, nuclear depletion, and death [S3]. Stress granules provide another sequestration route [S4], while C9orf72-repeat models implicate RanGAP-associated import defects [S2]. These are competing precedents, not evidence for phosphorylation-first disease. Unverified prior knowledge: CDK1-associated NUP98 phosphorylation contributes to mitotic pore disassembly. This target-specific precedent motivates the pair but does not establish neuronal plausibility by itself. Aberrant reactivation could reuse that pore-remodeling reaction without mitosis; this is explicitly assumed. No supplied source establishes relevant CDK1 activity in postmitotic motor neurons. Demonstrating that activity in verified noncycling neurons is therefore a prerequisite; its absence would reject this nomination rather than justify substituting another kinase.\n\nDisease specificity and timing. Follow several independent ALS and matched unaffected donor lines during unstressed maturation, reserving transient sublethal stress for a secondary arm. Predict an ALS-enriched sequence of CDK1 activation, NUP98 phosphorylation, pore displacement, import failure, and nuclear TDP-43 loss before transport-factor sequestration. Equivalent changes occurring only under matched stress would support generic remodeling, not ALS specificity. Serial imaging and matched-culture phosphoproteomics should measure import kinetics, pore-associated NUP98, TDP-43 localization, both assembly types, transport-factor capture—including soluble complexes—and later firing and survival. Monitor export, passive permeability, energy status, and stress responses to distinguish transport failure from cellular collapse.\n\nKinase and substitution controls. Identify NUP98 sites increasing before import failure, establish CDK1 dependence through acute genetic suppression with endogenous-level wild-type versus kinase-dead rescue, and test direct phosphorylation by purified CDK1. Freeze the site set before held-out-donor validation. Introduce inducible, endogenous-level phospho-resistant replacement after differentiation, with wild-type replacement/reversion controls and matched basal incorporation and function. Protection alone is insufficient: substitutions may confer stress-dependent structural stability unrelated to phosphorylation.\n\nOrthogonal causal tests. With wild-type NUP98, reversible NUP98-targeted CDK1 recruitment should induce disease-matched site occupancy, and targeted phosphatase recruitment should reverse it. Include catalytically inactive, untargeted, and recruitment-only controls; assay neighboring proteins for off-target phosphorylation. Induced phosphorylation should produce pore loss and import failure without preceding factor sequestration or increased general stress. To distinguish residence-mediated failure from other NUP98 functions, independently tether endogenous NUP98 reversibly to a pore scaffold. Import rescue while phosphorylation remains elevated would support residence as the mediator. Verify that tethering itself does not alter basal transport or permeability.\n\nAlternative-route discrimination. Use a three-factor design combining NUP98-phosphorylation blockade, conventional stress-granule suppression, and independent granule-independent TDP-43 assembly disruption. For the latter, develop a cytoplasm-restricted assembly-disrupting binder. This is an unvalidated reagent: require demonstrated reduction of assemblies and transport-factor capture while preserving baseline nuclear TDP-43 abundance/function, comparable stress, and NUP98 phosphorylation. If selectivity cannot be achieved, this alternative remains unresolved, not excluded by monitoring. Import rescue by phosphorylation blockade despite comparable sequestration favors intrinsic pore modification. Rescue only by blocking sequestration while the NUP98 lesion persists favors the competing explanation. Test downstream mediation separately: near-endogenous nuclear TDP-43 retention should improve firing/survival without correcting phosphorylation or early import failure.\n\nFalsification and limitations. Verified pre-onset blockade of nominated phosphorylation that preserves basal pore function but leaves early import failure unchanged falsifies the initiating claim. Phosphorylation consistently following import failure also contradicts initiation. Stress-challenge-only protection cannot establish spontaneous disease causation. Donor heterogeneity, neuronal immaturity, undetected sequestration, imperfect targeting, and structural effects of tethers or binders remain limitations. Even positive results would support this defined sporadic-ALS context, not a universal ALS mechanism or validated treatment.",
